Context
  Asked for subagents to grind the Python files, the contracts, CI/flags/params
  parsing, UI/UX, error handling and exit codes. One review agent returned twenty
  findings, each with a command that reproduces it.

Deviations
  Seven fixed, thirteen deferred. The thirteen, so they are not lost:

    corpus-sweep.sh word-splits $targets and swallows a bad directory (exit 0)
    corpus-sweep.sh GEISER column never checks the 3.x its own header promises
    ship-check.sh DIRECT heuristic false-positives on the correct quoted form
    audit.sh swallows an unresolvable BASE and audits an empty file list
    eval-suite.sh --probe is indistinguishable from a run once the gate opens
    sync-skill-scripts.sh reports only the first drift, not all
    validate-contracts.py: one bad artifact suppresses every later ok line
    validate-monitors.py: a missing skills/ silently passes on-skill-invoke
    hooks.json has no validated_by, so its recorded gap is untestable
    artifacts.json and the validators duplicate rules with no cross-check
    absolute vs repo-relative paths differ across the four validators
    only audit.sh and eval-suite.sh honour 2 = could-not-run
    none of the validators pass encoding= to read_text()

  The exit-code inconsistency is the one with the most leverage: three scripts use 1
  for could-not-run where two use 2, so a caller cannot distinguish failure from
  absence. That wants one decision applied everywhere rather than seven patches.

Considerations
  The review also confirmed things I had assumed: python3 is 3.11.15 so the union
  syntax is fine, there are no dead imports or silent excepts, shellcheck is clean
  apart from two annotated cases, and every Makefile target's argv matches its
  script's parsing except the two noted above.

Timeline
  2026-09-29, nexus, as dsp-dr. Seven fixes, each verified with the reviewer's own
  reproducing command; gmake checks green.
